<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Lunéville</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1801">1801</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-second-coalition">War of the Second Coalition</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneville">Lunéville</a> was signed on 9 February 1801 between the French Republic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Emperor Francis II</a> after Austrian defeats at Marengo and Hohenlinden. It confirmed the terms of Campo Formio, ceding the whole left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and recognising French satellite republics in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>. Princes who lost land west of the Rhine were compensated with church lands, leading to the reorganisation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> in 1803.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-luneville.jpg" alt="Treaty of Lunéville" width="320" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Strategic_Situation_of_Europe_1801.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>French armies occupied the left bank of the Rhine from 1794, and its annexation by France was recognised in the Treaty of Campo Formio in 1797 and the <b>Peace of Lunéville</b> in 1801.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>French troops occupied Tuscany in 1799, and after the <b>Treaty of Lunéville</b> it was turned into the Kingdom of Etruria for Louis of Bourbon-Parma in 1801.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Grand Duchy of Tuscany · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…Austrian Netherlands and Lombardy in return for Venice, and after defeats at Marengo and Hohenlinden in 1800 the <b>Treaty of Lunéville</b> of 1801 confirmed French control of the left bank of the Rhine and set in motion the secularisation of the German church states, which undermined the Holy…</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>French revolutionary troops occupied it in 1794, and it was formally annexed by France under the <b>Treaty of Lunéville</b> in 1801.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-aachen">Free Imperial City of Aachen</a></li><li><blockquote>Imperial claims in Italy ended with the French Revolutionary Wars and the <b>Treaty of Lunéville</b> in 1801.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-italy-medieval">Kingdom of Italy (medieval)</a></li></ul></section>
